#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078171 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078562 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49541737-272f-5821-8bf2-241989e14349" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing a Variable Fraction Using the Quotient Rule For Radicals"}] :question/id #uuid "3b907098-3e14-5db0-914f-811c84a79d1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-1385" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{8a}}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8a}} &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8a}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{8a}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{8a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8a}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{8a}}{\\sqrt{8a}} &=  \\\\\n(\\sqrt{24a})/((\\sqrt{8a})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{4 \\cdot 6a}}{8a} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2\\sqrt{6a}}{8a} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{6a}}{4a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078172 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078563 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd46dce5-3009-5b72-9230-f4b66fa77c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing a Variable Fraction With More Than Two Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "226ef796-a5c7-5113-a268-825bc1738a78" :question/math-academy-id "e-1386" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{\\sqrt{z+1}}$ without radicals in the denominator." :question/worked-solution "We need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{z+1}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{z+1}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{y} + 2}{\\sqrt{z + 1}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{y} + 2}{\\sqrt{z + 1}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{z + 1}}{\\sqrt{z + 1}} &=  \\\\\n((\\sqrt{y} + 2)\\sqrt{z + 1})/((\\sqrt{z + 1})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{y} \\cdot \\sqrt{z + 1} + 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{z + 1}}{z + 1} &=  \\\\\n(\\sqrt{y \\cdot (z + 1)} + 2\\sqrt{z + 1})/(z + 1) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{yz + y} + 2\\sqrt{z + 1}}{z + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078342 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "064cd24d-8e8c-5c55-b354-e84edb518c59" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing a Variable Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "b7506e51-659a-5f10-a2a8-822e8378768c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6718" :question/problem "Rationalize $\\dfrac{x}{2\\sqrt{y}}$." :question/worked-solution "We need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{y}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{y}$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{y}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x \\cdot \\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{y} \\cdot \\sqrt{y}} &=  \\\\\n(x\\sqrt{y})/(2(\\sqrt{y})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{x\\sqrt{y}}{2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
